WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.370
- [Reporter] These are voters
in Wisconsin waiting to cast

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.100
their ballot in the
April primary election,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.030
despite being under stay-at-home orders

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.200
during the coronavirus pandemic.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.390
With a potential resurgence of the virus

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.090
in the fall of 2020, many are
questioning how they will vote

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.930
in the November general election

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.980
without jeopardizing their health.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.130
One solution?

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.140
- Mail-in ballots.
- Mail ballots.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.810
- Vote-by-mail.
- Vote-by-mail.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.090
- [Reporter] Lawmakers are debating

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.680
whether expanded absentee voting policies

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.800
should be adopted in all 50 states.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.810
- It shouldn't be mail-in
voting, it should be you go

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:39.340
to a booth and you
proudly display yourself.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.810
- So, the real problem
right now is public safety

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.090
and that people shouldn't have to choose

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.060
between voting and their
health and we all know

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.550
there is one solution, which
is riding out in front of us

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.490
and that is voting at
home or voting by mail.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.400
- [Reporter] To understand
the debate, first,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.770
you have to understand what
voting by mail looks like now.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.380
All states allow forms of voting by mail

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.810
but for some, it has limitations.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.640
Currently, five states have
primarily mail-in elections.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.090
This includes Oregon,
which was the first state

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.110
to fully adopt the policy in 1998.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.840
- The states that have
a culture and history

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.220
of extensive use of vote-by-mail

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.840
have enjoyed higher turnout.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.490
The states that extensively
use vote-by-mail really like it

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.900
because they think it's
more convenient for voters,

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.500
they think it is cheaper
and easier to administer.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.250
- [Reporter] 29 states have what is called

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.370
no-excuse absentee
voting, which means that

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.110
any voter can request a
ballot to vote from home,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.350
which they can then mail in
or drop off in a ballot box.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:45.330
The remaining 16 states, which
you can see here in gray,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.800
allow for mail-in voting
but only with an excuse,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.550
like having a physical disability

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.063
or being away on election day.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.300
Some states, such as New
York, Maryland and Georgia,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.760
have already opted to
expand voting-by-mail

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.043
because of the pandemic.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.020
Some lawmakers are arguing
that absentee voting

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.530
should be accessible
and easy for all voters.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.030
Senators Amy Klobuchar and
Ron Wyden have introduced

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.140
the Natural Disaster and
Emergency Ballot Act.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.390
The bill would expand
early in-person voting

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.580
and no-excuse vote-by-mail
to all registered voters

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.480
in every state, among other things.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.360
- So, we don't know, as
much as we'd like to,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.140
what will happen in
November but the safe bet is

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.650
that this will still
be around in some form

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.210
and that we need to plan ahead

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.070
and allow people to vote safely at home.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.860
- [Reporter] In March, Congress passed

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.110
a $2.2 trillion coronavirus relief bill,

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.900
that included 400 million

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.483
to address voting issues
created by the pandemic.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.450
Speaker of the House
Nancy Pelosi is calling

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.810
for more funds in the next stimulus.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.030
- It has been estimated
by the Brennan Center

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.780
that it would take about
$2 billion to ensure

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.500
that the appropriate
policies can be in place

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.560
in order to make sure that

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.170
the November election
goes off without a hitch.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.050
- [Reporter] For all Americans
to have the full ability

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.390
to vote-by-mail in the 2020
election, the policy will need

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:12.010
to be included in legislation
that is approved by Congress.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.470
- There's huge urgency
to getting this bill done

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:15.990
because the Secretary of States

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.430
in each state need time to plan.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.740
- [Reporter] It will require
significant resources

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.760
for states to enact voting reforms

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.270
less than seven months before
a presidential election.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.910
This includes hiring
additional election staff

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.980
and purchasing equipment such
as high-speed ballot scanners

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.283
and large orders of envelopes and postage.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.910
The Brennan Center estimates
the total cost of postage

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:42.720
to be between 413 and 593 million dollars,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.330
while ballot drop boxes
with security could cost

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.163
an additional 117 to 165 million.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.460
- The states that extensively
use vote-by-mail have

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.810
a variety of security mechanisms.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.470
They start with voter rolls
that they believe are clean

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.070
because they have been doing
a number of interactions

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.440
with the voters and they
are constantly updating

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.930
the information that they
get, they have mechanisms

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.010
for tracking where the
ballot is in the pipeline

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.150
to ensure the chain of custody, they have

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:16.030
non-discriminatory procedures
for comparing signatures.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.840
- [Reporter] Opponents
of mail-in voting argue

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.850
that it increases the risk of fraud

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.220
and that it's not a reliable
way to cast a ballot.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.640
- The most secure way to do
an election where people have

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.620
the most confidence in the
election is to have people go to

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.860
an assigned polling place
on election day and vote.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.320
- [Reporter] They say that voting early

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.510
also encourages voters
to cast their ballots

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.410
before having all of the
information from a campaign.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.870
While researchers have
found cases of fraud,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.600
evidence shows that, overall, it's rare.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.270
The debate over vote-by-mail
doesn't always follow

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.550
party lines, some Republican
governors are pushing

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.400
for temporary voting-by-mail

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.060
just during the coronavirus pandemic.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.430
But for some states, the only
way to allow absentee voting

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.713
for all voters would be to
change the state constitution.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.670
Experts point out that it
may not be the best option

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.930
for everyone and that ensuring the safety

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.460
of voters will require multiple methods.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.050
- There are going to be some communities

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.030
that have terrible mail service

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.320
and it would be
inappropriate to expect them

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.300
to vote by mail when they
don't trust the mail.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.040
- It's not one size fits all,
we want to, as a country,

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.430
to move to mail-in balloting
but we know that people

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.850
with disabilities, many of
them, can't vote by mail,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.450
you'll have people that
do wanna vote at the polls

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.610
for some reason or don't have,
their ballot didn't arrive,

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.120
all kinds of things can happen.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.420
So, you don't wanna have
them lose that right.

